WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.110
Even in a benign AI scenario,&nbsp;
we will be left behind.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:12.390
Neuralink is Elon Musk's answer to the threat&nbsp;
he believes artificial intelligence poses to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:19.560
the human race. In many ways, we're like&nbsp;
cyborgs anyway because our smartphone is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.150
like an extension of us, like a limb. The&nbsp;
problem is the communication bandwidth to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:28.620
the phone is too slow. It's a speed issue.&nbsp;
We're getting the information to our brains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:34.440
too slowly and not sending it out fast enough.&nbsp;
You need something artificial in your brain to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:40.530
help with this process. Neuralink connects human&nbsp;
brains to computers through a chip smaller than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:45.390
penny. You insert it by drilling a small hole in&nbsp;
the skull although this could be as simple as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:51.180
LASIK procedure in the future. A robot implants&nbsp;
the device to ensure extreme precision. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:57.150
chip is connected to the brain through 1,000 tiny&nbsp;
wires that are ten times thinner than human hair.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:03.330
The company says these wires are flexible enough&nbsp;
not to damage brain tissue. This is key because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:08.010
other BMIs such as deep brain stimulation&nbsp;
which is used to treat Parkinson's insert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:13.860
much larger wires which carry more risks such as&nbsp;
strokes. So how does the Neuralink device work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:21.180
how does it enhance your brain? Your brain is made&nbsp;
up of neurons. 100 billion neurons. The neurons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:26.370
come together to form a large network through&nbsp;
synapses. At these connection points, neurons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:31.620
talk to each other using chemical signals called&nbsp;
neurotransmitters. Everything you see, hear,&nbsp;

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:37.230
taste, smell, feel - these are just neurons firing&nbsp;
or what's called an action potential. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:43.200
where the Neuralink chip comes in. The wires on&nbsp;
the chip carry electrodes. The electrodes record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.300
the signals produced by the action potential and&nbsp;
send them to an external device worn behind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:53.550
ear. The device is controlled by an app so the&nbsp;
electrodes basically read what is happening in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:59.310
the brain and repair the broken circuits. The&nbsp;
electrodes stimulate as many neurons as possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:04.770
in orders of magnitude greater than what's ever&nbsp;
been done before to increase the capabilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:09.260
of our brain. This will be life-changing&nbsp;
for people with brain disorders. Neuralink&nbsp;

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:13.820
says it will help people who are paralyzed&nbsp;
move, allow those who are blind to see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:19.580
treat Alzheimer's, cure epilepsy. When Elon&nbsp;
appeared on Joe Rogan's podcast, he said Neuralink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:24.860
could in principle fix anything that is wrong with&nbsp;
the brain and that the device could be implanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:30.590
into someone as part of a trial within a year.
It's already been tested on monkeys. A monkey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.380
has been able to control the&nbsp;
computer with his brain. Just fyi.

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:41.720
Neuralink is building on the shoulders of&nbsp;
other implant technologies. Matthew Nagle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:45.860
who was paralyzed after being stabbed in the&nbsp;
neck, was the first person to use a brain-machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:51.290
interface in 2006 that allowed him to control&nbsp;
a computer cursor. Even though Neuralink is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:56.300
focused on treating brain injuries for now, that&nbsp;
is just the starting point for the company. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:00.530
will be updated versions of Neuralink. Kind of&nbsp;
like a software update for your computer where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:05.540
the latest version could be capable of so much&nbsp;
more. So what are some things you could do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:10.910
a Neuralink device in the future? Elon says humans&nbsp;
will be able to recall everything like a movie and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:16.250
play it back. We'll have superhuman memory. If&nbsp;
you travel to a foreign country you'll be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:21.290
to download a program and speak in that language.&nbsp;
In fact, you wouldn't need to speak at all. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:26.750
could communicate with people without speaking if&nbsp;
you both have the implant. Elon says telepathic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:32.030
communication could happen in as little as&nbsp;
five to ten years although making deadlines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:36.980
is not his strong suit and he's admitted to this&nbsp;
himself. Neuralink is just one of many companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:42.320
thinking about brain-machine interfaces. Facebook&nbsp;
is funding research on BMIs that will allow you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:47.240
to type with just your mind. Google's parent&nbsp;
company Alphabet has a research organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:53.060
that wants to treat diseases with an implantable&nbsp;
device. The ultimate goal of Neuuralink is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:59.060
achieve a symbiosis with artificial intelligence.&nbsp;
A symbiosis is a mutually beneficial relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.920
between two parties in the natural world.&nbsp;
It's been used to describe the relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:08.720
between bees and plant pollen, for example.&nbsp;
In this context, it describes the way humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:14.510
and AI can develop and evolve together. But&nbsp;
merging with AI comes with dangers. When your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:18.830
computer gets hacked, that's a problem. When&nbsp;
it comes to your brain, that's a bigger issue.&nbsp;

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:24.070
Scientists have been urging governments to address&nbsp;
what data should be collected, how data will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:29.080
kept safe, how citizens can opt-out of having&nbsp;
their data shared as a default. Neuralink's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:33.640
products will have to be approved by the U.S.&nbsp;
Food and Drug Administration. Approval is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:38.470
easy to get. It will take a long time and you'll&nbsp;
see it coming. But if and when it is approved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:42.730
it can change your lives and I don't say&nbsp;
that lightly. It can change our lives in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:47.170
the way the internet has connected the world,&nbsp;
in the way airplanes have allowed us to travel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:51.700
in the way medicine has cured disease. We may&nbsp;
be looking at our world now and thinking that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:56.020
we're lucky to live in this day and age and that&nbsp;
two decades ago, they couldn't have imagined what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:00.640
would be possible. Yet two decades from now,&nbsp;
people will look at us and think about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:07.030
far behind we were. We ain't seen nothin' yet.
I'm Cindy Pom. You've been watching Newsthink.

